you're gonna be producing
other television.

00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:22.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
You got to work
with Amy Poehler in "Update.

00:08:22.068 --> 00:08:24.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
You guys managed that desk
didn't you?

00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:25.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:27.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was some of the best years
of my life.

00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:28.107 line:92%
-And now it's Jost and Che

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they're doing
such a good job.

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The best.
-It's just wonderful.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.411 line:92%
It wasn't a gig I enjoyed doing,

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:34.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
but when I saw people
who could really handle it

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:36.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
like you and Amy,
it was wonderful.

00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:38.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, you just -- you sort of
did it as a character.

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.152
Isn't that a safe way to say it?
-I did, and I --

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:41.988
And it didn't really fit.
I was trying to be straigh

00:08:41.988 --> 00:08:44.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
all the way through
and not accepting the jokes.

00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:46.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
You guys had it perfectly,
especially you.

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:49.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think probably
you were one of the best eve

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:51.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
to deliver that material.
And you've made it.

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:55.134
And you must have young people
always coming to you and saying,

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:56.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
"How do I get
into the business?"

00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:58.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
or, "How do I get
into the industry?"

00:08:58.404 --> 00:08:59.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
or, "How do I learn
about comedy?"

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:02.475
Well, I have -- tonight,
I've got some tips for you

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:03.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, great.
-For those young people

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:07.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
out there who want to lear
about comedy and improv

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:11.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
and stand-up, the first thin
that you have to do

00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:13.819 size:84% position:42%
is you've got to go the
National Comedy Museum

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:16.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Erie --
in Jamestown, New York.

00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:18.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's in the old
Erie Railway Station there

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:22.228
And it takes you right from,
you know, the Greek comedies -

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:25.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
Aristophanes, "The Frogs" --
all the way through

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:26.832 line:92%
the blue comedy of the '50s.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:31.070
You know, those blue records
that women and men were making

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
right through
to "Saturday Night Live"

00:09:33.806 --> 00:09:34.440 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and "Second City."

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is a museum of comedy
and not to be missed.

00:09:37.677 --> 00:09:39.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
And any kid in high school
or college

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:42.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
that wants to be a writer,
a comedy writer, they go there

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:47.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
they're gonna get an afternoon
course in comedy history.

00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:50.790
And my motorcycle is there
the motorcycle that I rode

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.293
to "SNL" on every four --
each of those four years.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:55.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
I rode down
the New York Thruway,

00:09:55.828 --> 00:09:58.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
taking my life in my hands
on that Harley,

00:09:58.331 --> 00:09:59.165 line:92%
and they came to me and said

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:03.002
"is there any memorabilia we can
get from your time at 'SNL'?

00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:05.705
I said, "The bike that kept me
alive for those four years."

00:10:05.705 --> 00:10:06.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
-So that's there,

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:08.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the story's there.
The other thing is,

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:13.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's an author
called Kliph Nesteroff.

00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:15.081 size:75% position:38% line:92%
N-E-S-T-E-R-H-O-F-F.

00:10:15.081 --> 00:10:16.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
He wrote a book called
"The Comedians."

00:10:16.549 --> 00:10:21.887
It's a manual for understandin
British and American comedy,

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:23.055 line:92%
the influence of Canadians

00:10:23.055 --> 00:10:27.627
It's one of the best compendiums
of comedy that I've ever read.

00:10:27.627 --> 00:10:31.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then Steve Martin's book
"Born Standing Up,"

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:33.633 line:92%
and his book "The Troubadour."

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:36.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then John Lithgow
wrote a book called "Drama."

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:40.006
And so any novice there that
wants to get into the industry

00:10:40.006 --> 00:10:42.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
wants to understand rhythm
comedy, performance,

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:44.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
the commitment of an actor
in comedy,

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.312
I would say those are the book
to read -- "Born Standing Up,"

00:10:46.312 --> 00:10:49.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Troubadour," Nesteroff's book
"The Comedian,"

00:10:49.448 --> 00:10:51.984 align:start size:94% position:6%
and get to the museum
in Jamestown.

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:55.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's there because
Lucille Ball's home was there.

00:10:55.721 --> 00:11:00.192
-That's fantastic advice.
So kind of you to pass it along.

00:11:00.192 --> 00:11:01.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for being here, Dan.

00:11:01.761 --> 00:11:03.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, absolutely.
-It is always such a pleasure.

00:11:03.763 --> 00:11:06.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
Crystal Head Onyx
is available now.

